cmdlets follow a Verb-Noun naming convention
    (YES FINALLY A NAMING CONVENTION)
Get-<thing> - Retrieves something
    Get-Content - Retrieves content of a file
                and displays it to the console
Set-<Thing> - Changes something
    Set-Location - Changes CWD
use Get-Command to see a list of all available    cmdlets
alongside that, you can use -CommandType "<str>"
    to specify a specific type
Get-Help is similar to man pages

Aliases allow certain cmdlets to utilize their 
    cmd/bash counterpart
    ex:
    Set-Location can be called with cd
    Get-ChildItem can be called with dir or ls
    Get-Content can be called with type or cat

Get-process - eq to ps
Get-Service - same as tasklist or ps/top
Get-NetTCPConnection - Netstat
Get-FileHash - useful for integrity verification

Invoke-Command - enables efficient remote management
    and automation of tasks across multiple services
    Can be used to execute payloads or commands
    on target systems during an engagement

